1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,770
Hello and welcome back.

2
00:00:02,530 --> 00:00:07,550
What if I told you that Elasticsearch now makes semantic search as simple as writing a regular text query?

3
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:14,650
Today we're going to journey through the evolution of search, from traditional keyword matching to modern AI-powered semantic understanding.

4
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:21,370
I'll touch upon some important milestones on the way and recommend best practices to feature specific search use cases.

5
00:00:21,730 --> 00:00:25,430
We'll use a sample e-commerce data set to compare different search methods,

6
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:30,030
and I'll show you why ultimately the semantic text field type is about the change.

7
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:32,210
how you think about search implementation.

8
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:37,010
Spoiler alert, it's ridiculously simple compared to what we used to do.

9
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:39,850
By the end of this video, you'll understand why.

10
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:41,170
Let's dive in.

11
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:45,510
Let's start with the baseline, traditional lexical search using BM25.

12
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:48,430
This is what most of us have been using for years.

13
00:00:49,090 --> 00:00:52,690
I've got an e-commerce data set with over 2,500 products loaded in.

14
00:00:53,310 --> 00:00:57,910
Let's search for comfortable furniture for a large balcony using a simple match query.

15
00:00:58,590 --> 00:00:59,830
And here are results.

16
00:00:59,830 --> 00:01:03,540
The top hit is a Barbie dream house.

17
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:07,440
So that's not exactly what I had in mind for my balcony.

18
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:11,630
This happened because lexical search matches exact keywords.

19
00:01:12,190 --> 00:01:15,650
The Barbie dream house has furniture in its description, so it matched.

20
00:01:16,230 --> 00:01:18,510
But sadly, I won't fit in a Barbie lounger.

21
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:24,540
Lexical search is fast and simple, but it suffers from vocabulary mismatch.

22
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:29,680
It finds exact word matches, but misses the meaning behind what we're actually looking for.

23
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:33,640
What if we want semantic understanding, not just keyword matching?

24
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,180
Enter semantic search with dense and sparse vectors.

25
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:42,420
Released in Elasticse Search 8.4, this approach uses embedding models to understand meaning.

26
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:45,380
But wow, look at all this setup complexity.

27
00:01:46,330 --> 00:01:48,430
Find the right embedding model for your use case.

28
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:53,830
Then make sure you create an index mapping of the vector with the correct dimensions and similarity algorithm.

29
00:01:54,230 --> 00:01:58,510
This method also required us to configure inference pipelines, if not done locally.

30
00:01:59,170 --> 00:01:59,690
Create a configuration.

31
00:01:59,710 --> 00:02:01,590
to receive incoming data,

32
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:04,710
and assign an embedding model to convert the data into a vector.

33
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:08,850
Make sure you have no compute power to do this, otherwise you'll suffer from latency.

34
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:10,850
And look at this query structure.

35
00:02:11,390 --> 00:02:12,210
Just look at it.

36
00:02:12,690 --> 00:02:18,410
You need to understand vector fields, embedding models, and then configure candidates and similarity algorithms.

37
00:02:19,050 --> 00:02:19,830
But here's the thing.

38
00:02:20,330 --> 00:02:21,930
The results are actually much better.

39
00:02:22,530 --> 00:02:27,310
We're now finding outdoor patio furniture without the word outdoor even being in our query.

40
00:02:27,810 --> 00:02:29,690
The model understands that BALPATO furniture,

41
00:02:29,690 --> 00:02:32,310
Furniture typically means outdoor furniture.

42
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:33,350
The catch?

43
00:02:34,010 --> 00:02:36,330
This was way too complex for most use cases.

44
00:02:36,850 --> 00:02:41,530
You're doing all the heavy lifting, managing models, handling vectors, writing complex queries.

45
00:02:41,970 --> 00:02:42,990
There has to be a better way.

46
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:44,330
But fear not.

47
00:02:45,050 --> 00:02:51,050
Our recent developments have addressed all of this, and inference pipelines and complex mappings are virtually a thing of the past.

48
00:02:51,850 --> 00:02:52,590
And here it is.

49
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:53,870
The Game Changer.

50
00:02:54,470 --> 00:02:55,990
Meet Semantic Text.

51
00:02:56,990 --> 00:02:59,670
Elasticsearch 8.15's newest field type that

52
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:02,630
that makes semantic search as simple as regular text search.

53
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:04,370
Let me show you the difference.

54
00:03:05,390 --> 00:03:07,930
Just change your field type in query like normal text.

55
00:03:08,490 --> 00:03:09,410
That's it for the mapping.

56
00:03:10,210 --> 00:03:12,690
Just specify the field type in which model to use.

57
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:16,370
Elasticsearch handles all the vector magic behind the scenes.

58
00:03:16,990 --> 00:03:19,950
It automatically embeds text data you store in the field.

59
00:03:20,710 --> 00:03:25,430
No more inference pipelines to configure and manage and no more machine learning nodes to monitor.

60
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:29,450
The most recent addition to the Elasticsearch text embedding model list,

61
00:03:29,690 --> 00:03:33,370
is Gina Embedding's V5 running on the Elastic Inferred Service,

62
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:38,810
which makes it easy for developers to add fast, high quality, and reliable semantic retrieval

63
00:03:38,810 --> 00:03:41,550
for search, rag, and agentic applications.

64
00:03:42,550 --> 00:03:47,190
EIS offers fully managed GPU-powered inference with ready-to-use models,

65
00:03:47,710 --> 00:03:50,170
no additional setup or hosting complexity required.

66
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:52,530
Oh, and here's the query.

67
00:03:53,230 --> 00:03:55,550
Clean, readable, and intuitive.

68
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:59,670
No more vector dimensions, no more complex K&N structure,

69
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:01,790
But here's the beautiful part.

70
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:04,910
We get the exact same high quality semantic results.

71
00:04:05,570 --> 00:04:10,070
Same model understanding, same relevance, but with zero complexity on your end.

72
00:04:10,790 --> 00:04:11,730
The benefits are huge.

73
00:04:12,270 --> 00:04:13,490
Automatic model management.

74
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:15,450
No pipeline setup required.

75
00:04:15,930 --> 00:04:18,930
Clean queries and the same great semantic results.

76
00:04:19,390 --> 00:04:22,730
It's like having a semantic search expert built into your field type.

77
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:26,920
But there's another approach that might just be the perfect solution.

78
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:28,560
Hybrid search.

79
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:29,700
The best of both.

80
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:32,980
worlds was released in Elasticsearch 8.16.

81
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:39,020
What if you could get lexical precision and semantic understanding in a single search?

82
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:46,060
This uses something called reciprocal rank fusion, or RRF, released in Elasticsearch 8.16.

83
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:51,540
Think of it as a smart referee that takes results from both lexical and semantic search,

84
00:04:51,920 --> 00:04:55,760
then combines them into one ranked list based on what performs best.

85
00:04:56,340 --> 00:04:59,800
Once those two results sets are merged, you can use a semantic

86
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:01,280
re-ranker.

87
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:03,860
Semantic re-rankers use machine learning models,

88
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:07,280
such as our cross-encoder models like Gina Rer V3,

89
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:11,640
to reorder search results based on their semantic similarity to a query.

90
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:16,100
Released with full endpoint support in Elasticsearchase 8.18,

91
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:20,340
this technique operates in real-time on a small top-k results set

92
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:25,300
and can be integrated into search retrieval methods like hybrid search using RRF.

93
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:28,900
An example query structure shows a text similarity re-ranker

94
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:29,960
can wrap an RRRF.

95
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:37,120
RRF Retriever, incorporating semantic search and keyword search before AI re-ranking the top results in a single API call.

96
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:41,440
Here's what's amazing. The query structure is still simple.

97
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:47,260
You're just defining two retrievers, one for exact keyword matching and one for semantic understanding,

98
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:50,420
and wrapping them up in two ranking modifiers.

99
00:05:50,940 --> 00:05:55,480
The results are incredible. You get the precision of exact matches when they matter,

100
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:58,980
plus the intelligence of semantic search for broader understanding.

101
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:03,670
No complex vector management, no cluttered response to clean up.

102
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:09,400
When would you use hybrid search? There's a lot of examples we can think of with our e-commerce item list.

103
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:16,320
Product catalogs where users might search for exact model numbers or descriptive needs like waterproof hiking boots.

104
00:06:17,130 --> 00:06:28,050
Parts look up where brake pad Mazda CX5 2016 needs exact matching, but replacement parts for sensible U.S. crossover needs semantic understanding.

105
00:06:28,370 --> 00:06:36,770
Fashion retail, where red dress size 8, requires precision, but elegant evening wear, needs contextual understanding.

106
00:06:37,370 --> 00:06:42,670
This is perfect for production environments where you want comprehensive search coverage without the complexity.

107
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:46,130
Lastly, what if you prefer SQL-style queries?

108
00:06:46,710 --> 00:06:49,990
Meet ESQL, released in Elasticsearch 8.14.

109
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:54,630
This is a SQL-like query language, and it works beautifully with semantic text.

110
00:06:54,890 --> 00:06:56,470
Look how natural this is.

111
00:06:56,470 --> 00:07:02,350
ESQL automatically detects the semantic text field type and run semantic search.

112
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:07,350
You see text, but underneath, it's leveraging vectors for semantic understanding.

113
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:12,000
Same great semantic results, but now in a familiar SQL syntax.

114
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:16,620
This is perfect when you need complex data manipulation alongside search,

115
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:19,020
or when your team is more comfortable with SQL.

116
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:25,020
Think analytics, reporting, or complex data transformations combined with semantic search,

117
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:26,480
all in one query.

118
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:32,470
language. So here's my recommendation based on your needs. Start with semantic search.

119
00:07:33,390 --> 00:07:39,050
It gives you 80% of the benefits with 10% of the complexity. Clean, simple, and powerful.

120
00:07:39,930 --> 00:07:46,730
Level up with hybrid search using RRF. Combines semantic text with lexical search for comprehensive coverage.

121
00:07:47,470 --> 00:07:51,190
You get exact matches when they matter and semantic understanding when you need it.

122
00:07:52,050 --> 00:07:56,550
Maximize relevance with a Ranking. Add a text similarity Reranker on

123
00:07:56,570 --> 00:08:01,070
top of your hybrid search to intelligently reorder results based on semantic similarity.

124
00:08:01,910 --> 00:08:07,010
This gives you the most sophisticated search experience in a single, straightforward API call.

125
00:08:07,730 --> 00:08:13,970
Remember, the combination of hybrid search with RRF and semantic re-ranking is the future of search.

126
00:08:14,290 --> 00:08:17,610
It's simple, effective, and it just works.

127
